ally deserve to live  safely and securely to enjoy equal  measures of freedom opportunity  and democracy to be treated with dignity  and in that press conference netanyahu  warned israel will have a powerful  response if hamas  breaks the ceasefire we discussed  also how to improve  the lives and the conditions of the  palestinians the humanitarian conditions  in gaza  including the question of the return of  our  mias and two civilians who are there as  for peace itself with the palestinians a  formal piece i think president biden was  absolutely correct when he said  you're not going to get peace until  israel is recognized as an independent  jewish state  and that is the key i couldn't agree  more  our diplomatic editor james base is  following anthony blinken's trip he  joins us now  live from ramallah so james we heard  that uh speech a short while ago  or that address along with the  palestinian president what does it all  boil down to what sort of  biden middle east policies emerging here  well certainly the palestinian authority  now have someone to deal with  they're they're talking again to the  united states and this is the first  high-level meeting with the secretary of  state for  well over three years so for them that's  good news  but then look at what has actually been  achieved during this visit well  uh there is the promise maybe of help in  the future with reconstruction of gaza  there's a package of aid of 260 million  dollars which in  global scheme of things is not a large  amount of money and much of that  has already been announced there is the  decision to reopen  the u.s consulate general in jerusalem  let me just explain that  there is a there was a u.s embassy in  tel aviv that dealt with israel and the  u.s consulate general in east jerusalem  that dealt with the palestinians  president trump merged the two  and also moved the u.s embassy to  jerusalem now  they are going to have a consulate  general again a diplomatic  um representation